    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="default"/>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts3" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts12">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hey, everybody, welcome back. In this video, we're going to give you five tips for doing public speaking with confidence, especially if you are introverted. And to do this, I have a special guest, Shelly, from the YouTube channel Shelly Saves the Day. Say hi, Shelly.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a2"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts17" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts36">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Shelly does live streaming from her channel. You can check it out. I will put a link to her channel in the description below this. And I have invited Iran to talk about public speaking today because about a month ago, I saw her do a fantastic presentation. And I thought, since she has admitted to me that she's an extreme introvert and I'm an extrovert, I thought we could bring on a genuine introvert for you, viewers, so you can get her perspective.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a3"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts37" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts38">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>work at Amazon, Google, and she now works at TubeBuddy.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a4"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts41" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts52">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Which, if you're in the YouTube world, you probably know what that is. It's a company that YouTubers use the app a lot for their channels. And I was wondering before we get into the meat of it, Shelly, if you could just tell us in your professional journey in those different positions, what does your public speaking journey look like to lead you to where you are right now?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a6"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts95" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts127">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, well, you're a great public speaker. I saw you do that presentation and it was really impressive. And it doesn't surprise me that they continue to push you out there. But it's funny that your journey is not unlike many people's journey. They go to school for whatever and they get into a career for something, maybe technical, and they don't think they're going to be doing public speaking. And that's probably why they didn't go into it in the first place. But you show a little competence and they say, hey, why don't you get up there and explain it at our next meeting, Shelly? And then there you go. Your journey just began. And here you are now, in some ways, almost like a professional speaker. You are out there at conferences in front of big audiences, speaking professionally, representing your company. I'm sure if someone had asked you.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a423"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts128" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts129">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a8"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts133" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts142">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And a lot of people, a lot of people watching are going to be in that situation before they know it. You're going to do a good job at something, and someone's going to say, go explain that to the whole team. And there you go</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a11"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts159" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts166">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So as an extreme introvert, which you have admitted to be, what overall big picture piece of advice would you give introverts who either want to do public speaking or like you were expected to do it?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a15"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts205" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts236">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh, that's so powerful. I think so much of it, at least 50% of people's public speaking problems are basically in their own head and nobody ever even knows that it's a problem for them. Yeah. So I have five things I noticed about your presentation the other day in Albany. And I was wondering if we could turn these into talking points for tips for our viewers. So I'll give an overview here and then we'll dig down for about a minute on each. I noticed that you were authentic. You had great. engagement with the audience. You delivered value in your message. You kept it conversational and you were very persuasive without feeling like you were giving us a sales pitch. So let's start with the first one. I thought you came across as very authentic.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a430"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts238" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts241">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>What is your mindset going?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a432"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts242" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts243">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>What's your mindset going in?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a433"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts244" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts245">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>What advice would you give to people about authenticity?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a20"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts281" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts290">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>No, it's so powerful because it's one less thing to think about, right? If I don't have to be a character, I don't have to pretend I can just focus on who I am, and then you can get down to the real work of delivering your message. So I also noticed you had excellent engagement, meaning you got us as the audience involved.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a21"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts291" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts302">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>How did you decide to do that? And I remember example, you probably remember the example I'm thinking of about how you got us all fired up in there. But how did you make that decision to get us involved rather than just talking at us?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a23"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts369" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts392">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, that's great. I do that in the classroom as a teacher. Of course, I'm always trying to get hands-on instead of just lecturing. But your example was fantastic. You used images that engages people. You asked us really clear questions, which one do you think is better? And then you had us guessing, you know, talking about it amongst ourselves and voting. I think you even had us raise our hands, right? That's engagement. And then, of course, you revealed in most cases that a lot of us were wrong. And that was kind of like, wow, you know, that was fascinating to us how we could be so wrong since we sort of do this professionally.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a25"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts400" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts408">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, you had the answers ahead of time. You're also delivering value. That next point, you did a great job.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a26"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts409" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts432">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And kept our attention because you were giving us something that we needed. And I think we talk about this in video. We say, you gotta, if people are clicking and watching a video, they're investing their time. So you better give them some value, give them something that they need. But we don't talk enough about that in the public speaking world. We just say, you know, do a good job. try to analyze your audience and tailor your message to them. But we don't talk about delivering value, but you did in your presentation. You gave us something we needed. So can you talk a little bit about your mindset in crafting that message so it helped us?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a28"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts483" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts492">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh, that's so great. So we have this triangle in the field of communication.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a29"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts493" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts496">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Speaker, audience, message, and what you're doing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a30"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts498" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts518">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>There in that triangle, you're not really thinking about yourself very much. You're thinking about your audience and your message and how you can connect those two for maximum impact. And I think that puts our ego in the back seat and it puts the focus where it should be on our listeners. So that's really great. Next thing you did really well was you were very conversational. It never felt like a lecture or that you were memorizing everything. It never felt stiff or wooden. How do you keep it conversational?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a441"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts576" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts577">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a32"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts585" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts592">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, and you adapt it all along the way. Like you're saying, if people come in with a word-for-word script, they can't do any of the things you just mentioned really, because they're stuck. And so you had talking points, you had slides, but you didn't have, I don't remember anything that sounded word-for-word.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a35"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts607" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts618">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, it sounds like reading. And who wants to hear you read your paper? You know, nobody wants to hear a student in class, for example, read their student paper. They want to just have a conversation. And the fifth point, our last point, is that you were really persuasive. I was persuaded, but you weren't, it never really felt like a sales pitch. It just felt like.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a36"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts619" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts626">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>You believed in the TubeBuddy app and you brought us along. So, tell us about your mindset. How are you persuasive without feeling like you're a salesperson?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a446"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts665" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts666">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a40"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts680" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts754">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Exactly. That's what it felt like. I was like, I couldn't believe it. I was like, I am absolutely. And I did. I went right upstairs, got on my laptop, and I ordered the advanced version. I've been using TubeBuddy forever. And for those who don't know, it's an app. That's all you need to know. But YouTubers use it. I've been using it forever, but there was another level. And I was happy to pay that because now one of the extra features is that I can translate all of my subtitles on my videos into numerous languages. And since that conference, all the new videos are being translated into about 12 different languages. So thank you for that, Shelly. You definitely earned your pay that day, but it never felt like a sales pitch. You just were solving my problem. And it was such a natural outcome of a great message demoing the product and helping me feel like I was excited to do it. And so for viewers, if you're watching, sometimes you're going to speak to inform people. Sometimes you're just training them, for example, the how-to speech, giving them some information. Sometimes you have to persuade them as part of your job. You have to bring people from A to B. You have to convince people to follow you. And that's where persuasive speaking comes in. That's really where you can level up your public speaking and presentation skills. If you can speak persuasively without feeling like you're pitching somebody and pushing them in a direction they don't want to go, that's a really great way to do that. So those are our five tips for introverts. Shelly's an introvert, but really all of these tips work for just about anybody. So I really appreciate your time, Shelly. Thanks for being here. And I just want to give your channel a shout out one more time. It's called Shelly Saves the Day. She has weekly live streams. It's really fun. I will put a link to that in the description below the video. Thanks, Shelly. Anything you want to say before we leave?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a42"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts763" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts772">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Excellent. Thanks so much for being here. See you everybody. We'll see you soon.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="category">
