    </TIER>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_01">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a421"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts14" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts16">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hey, everyone.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a422"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts53" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts92">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>It's so interesting because, as an introvert, sometimes one of the things I don't want to do is have to talk to a lot of people or do a lot of public speaking. And yet, in every single position I've been in, I've been in a graduated type of experience, having to talk to more people and on larger stages. It's a little terrifying, frankly. But when I was at Amazon, you know, I would speak at a women in tech conference as a speaker about my experiences. And I remember looking down into the audience and seeing our VP of our org as I'm berating them about what an experience it is for women in technology and in our particular org. So that's terrifying. And then when I went on to Google and I would go on and have to do public speaking, I was running meetings for our executive level people. And I was like lowest person on the totem pole being like, what am I doing here? Why am I telling them what to do and what not do? And then, you know, now that I work for TubeBuddy, it's so interesting because now I go to conferences and I speak on stages about how to do more things or how to be, you know, on public-facing stages. And it's terrifying. And it's like all of these milestones graduated stages. And I'm like, why, why am I doing this? But they keep asking me to do it. And I keep saying yes.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a424"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts126" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts132">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>It's heart palpitations to even like hear you say that. Like, I mean, technically, yes, but I'm like, oh, no.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a425"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts145" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts150">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>You're nailed it right there when you said competence apparently is confidence to other people. And so they take someone who knows something.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a426"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts151" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts158">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>about whatever it is that they want to know about and then says, oh, you seem to know about it. Go tell everyone. And then you're like, but, but, but, and they're not looking for that. They're just like, just tell us what you know.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a427"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts167" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts174">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE> I was going to say, I don't know if any of us like want to do it. We get forced or asked or voluntold to do it. And it's a whole other story. You know, we don't really always get to say no to that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a429"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts175" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts196">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>But here's the thing is I would say it's never going to be worse than it is in your own head. And technically, like you're not going to get up there, have a heart attack and fall over and die. That's usually, that's never usually going to be the case. It's usually going to be, I remember my biggest fear was saying, you know, is someone going to ask me a question that I don't know the answer to? And am I going to look and feel dumb and unprepared? And that's the worst case scenario in my mind, right? And so that's where you think.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a428"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts197" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts204">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>You build it all up in your head. And so I would say it's never any worse in person than it is in your own head. And so the biggest thing you have to get over is like changing your mindset around it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a431"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts239" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts240">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[inaudible]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a434"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts246" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts264">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Authenticity is a lot less exhausting than always having to put on a character. You don't have to remember, like, am I like sassy? Am I funny? Am I whatever? Like, you can be what you are, and you can just be that, and it's enough. And so, I think a lot of people go on and they have a completely different thing that happens to them. And then they get nervous because then, if anyone also asks a question afterwards or comes up to you, it's like, do I have to be that person or do I have to be this person? And if you're just yourself,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a435"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts265" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts278">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>It's always the same. So it's actually calming to know that like, no matter what version you're going to get of you, it's true and authentic. So I would say do that. Cause, you know, a lot of people will be like, fake it till you make it. And be like, or you can just be scared and terrified that you're not that person. So just be yourself. I know it's like mom advice, but like, just be yourself.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a436"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts294" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts295">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a437"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts305" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts366">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So some of it's kind of selfish in a way, because as an introvert, I didn't want to fill the time with just myself talking. So it's really, really easy to almost involve people and then be like, hey, what do you think? And then just for that 30 seconds, I could hear the room talking about like, oh, I'd pick this one or this one's better. And I don't have to say anything. I can agree. I can just nod. I can be like, uh-huh. I can, you know, I already know in my head how it's going to play out. So I kind of can, you know, chuckle to myself that everyone has already guessed wrong. And so that's. a really interesting position because like nothing is needed of you of that particular moment and you can just be like hey what do you think and having that little piece of conversation right and like everyone is going to have an opinion so your example was i put two pictures up of two different thumbnails on the screen and i would ask people one of these performed a lot better than the other one which one do you think it is and you leave it open and someone would be like this one and then be like okay well everyone raise your hand if you think it's this one or that one and as you start to build that participation it's like oh wow they're listening to me they're interacting with me they're engaging with me and wow look at this we're forming a relationship they're not so scary they're not going to kill me right all of that is going through your head and so you feel more comfortable because it's just easy</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a438"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts393" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts401">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Being in like the know, like you know what's gonna happen and that little tickle of like, oh, they all got it wrong. Just wait.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a439"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts435" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts482">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, so one thing that I sometimes think about is going to be, you know, that they're never going to remember the words, but they'll remember what you said or that feeling that you get. So they may not remember everything that I talked about, but they remember they had a good time, they laughed, they learned. It was like something, something stood out differently for other people at different times. It was like what they were ready to receive and hear at that point. It came into their lives when they needed it. And so if you think about that, it also becomes really easy to talk about whatever it is because you're like, this is not about me. This is not about how nervous I am. This is about, let's see, in this particular context, how can, you know, creators get the most out of this tool or these types of things, or how can we help them? And it becomes the end goal of, I just want to help you guys get better. And when you come from this very authentic, wanting to help type of place as well, it becomes really easy to engage and not have it be about you, but have it be about the message and the deliverable about this is what I want you to walk away from this with. This is what I want you to feel or how I want you to feel or like how you're going to feel empowered when you leave this. So that's what I really try and craft because it's not really about me. It's about that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a440"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts519" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts584">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So I think you try and build in some of those moments. So there were some moments where I was kind of vulnerable and I said, this has happened to me in the past. And like, that's a whole other story. And that is very true to my authentic self. I have a very squirrel brain. I usually have post-its. I will go off on all these side tangents. So I threw in a, but that's a whole other story for another day type of moment in my speech. And so, because that's how I speak and that's my authentic self saying that. So when I talk about that and when I ask you, what kind of thing would you do? What kind of thing do you see? That is because I want to know, because I'm really curious, genuinely curious, because it's not always about like what I can force onto you, because it's like, if this isn't a solution that's going to work for you, then I don't need to waste your time listening to or like pushing this on you. And I don't want to push anything. I want to be like, if this isn't beneficial for you, then I want to know that. But you have to know through conversation what it is, like how people react or what they actually want to see from you. And it's actually a lot easier than you think because you can read body language and you can just kind of hear like what's the intonation like you could tell that room was very like happy and jovial and like joking with each other and you know or like that's where I knew it was like safe to kind of yell at you in the audience and like kind of come back at you and like some rooms you can read it and you know that it's not it's not that type of room and so I think it's actually if you are intuitive and kind of introverted we a lot of time we're quiet but we're observant and so if you're kind of observant in the room it's really easy to kind of pick up some of those signals and then kind of interweave it and make it easier to be conversational</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a442"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts595" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts598">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I do bullet points. I have a harder time with like scripts because then I'll focus so much on trying to.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a443"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts599" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts604">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Read the words correctly without stuttering, or the intonation and inflection then goes and it doesn't seem very conversational. It seems robotic, like you're reading a script.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a444"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts616" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts617">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a445"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts627" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts658">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So such a good question because I think one of the biggest points is that we don't want to come across as sleazy, salesy, like persuasive too much in the wrong way. And that a lot of the time will stem from, I haven't provided enough value. I haven't shown you enough empirical evidence. I haven't like actually had a relationship with you because it's really easy if you're talking with your friend to be like, oh, you really need to check this out. Like it's not a sales pitch. It's a, oh, it's a trusted recommendation from a friend. And so I think a lot of the fear and nervousness that comes from feeling salesy and sleazy is either if you don't strongly believe in your product, if you know you don't have the rapport and relationship with an audience or a person, and you're trying to shove a solution into what isn't yet a problem for them. You know, so if I come by and I say like, this is the thing and they're like, but that's not my problem at all. They're like, yes, right. So.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a447"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts659" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts664">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>That's the thing when I talk about, you know, you said, Hey, I've got language translations I'd like to do. And I say, Hey, we have this thing that can do that. It's not all of a sudden like.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a448"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts667" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts681">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Someone who never asked and then saying like, hey, you need to do this so you can do the, you know, X, Y, and Z. It was like, oh, this is actual problem that I need and it will do it. It's not a sale at that point. It's a, it's just like, oh my gosh, please help me. Where have you been? Why, why didn't you tell me sooner? You know, exactly.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a449"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts755" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts762">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>You can do it, it's a skill, you just have to keep doing it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="transcribe">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a85"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts2" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts4">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hey everybody, welcome back in this video, we're going to give you five tips for doing</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
